---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Is a file still standard? The 3 criteria every manager must master"
  description="Complexity, amount, risk: three simple criteria that tell you when a file must leave standard handling and go up for approval. A practical PerfEco guide."
>
  <Nav langAlternate="/ressources/qualification-dossier-escalade-management" />
  <main class="pt-16">

    <!-- Hero article -->
    <section class="bg-navy text-white py-20 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <span class="text-xs bg-teal/20 text-white font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Governance</span>
          <span class="text-xs bg-teal/20 text-white font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Management</span>
          <time class="text-xs text-gray-200" datetime="2026-06-23">Week of 23 June 2026</time>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold mb-6 leading-snug">
          Is a file still standard?<br>The 3 criteria every manager must master
        </h1>
        <p class="text-gray-200 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          A file stops being standard the moment it exceeds the usual framework in complexity, in amount or in risk. Three simple criteria — and organisations pay dearly when they are left unclear.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Article body -->
    <section class="py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-lg prose-gray">

        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          In a high-performing organisation, the question is not only "who decides?" — it is first "how do we know a decision needs a stronger approval?". Without a clear framework, teams under-escalate what should go up, or over-escalate what they could handle on their own. Both are expensive.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">In short</p>
          <ul class="text-gray-700 space-y-1">
            <li><strong>Standard</strong> = known, simple, low risk — routine handling</li>
            <li><strong>Complex</strong> = judgement required — a manager must look at it</li>
            <li><strong>Risky</strong> = stronger approval — traceability and an explicit sign-off</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>The 3 main criteria</h2>

        <h3>1. The level of complexity</h3>
        <p>
          A file is no longer standard as soon as it carries any one of these:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Several special cases nested inside one another</li>
          <li>An unusual situation, with no clear precedent</li>
          <li>Incomplete information that forces someone to interpret</li>
          <li>Several departments involved, with potentially diverging interests</li>
          <li>A rule that is hard to interpret in this case</li>
          <li>An explicit exception to the usual rule</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The practical rule: <strong>if the person handling it hesitates, that is already a signal</strong>. Hesitation says the file goes beyond what can be handled alone without risk of error.
        </p>

        <h3>2. The amounts involved</h3>
        <p>
          The higher the financial commitment, the more the decision has to be secured. The rule is simple — but it assumes the organisation has set explicit thresholds:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A significant financial commitment (above the delegation threshold)</li>
          <li>A large commercial discount outside the standard pricing policy</li>
          <li>A disputed invoice above a certain amount</li>
          <li>A material risk of loss or credit note</li>
          <li>A measurable budget impact on the financial year</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Trigger thresholds must be <strong>documented, known and stable</strong>. A manager should never have to guess at which amount they are expected to consult their own manager.
        </p>

        <h3>3. The level of risk carried</h3>
        <p>
          A file leaves standard handling as soon as it carries an identifiable risk — even a potential one:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Quality or safety risk (for customers, teams, products)</li>
          <li>Regulatory or legal compliance risk</li>
          <li>Financial risk, or risk of direct loss</li>
          <li>Reputational risk (the organisation's standing)</li>
          <li>Strategic customer risk (an important commercial relationship or partnership)</li>
          <li>Operational risk (service continuity, critical dependencies)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The logic is simple: <strong>the higher the risk, the more the decision must be recorded and explicitly approved</strong>. This is not about distrusting teams — it is a condition of organisational resilience.
        </p>

        <h2>The additional signals</h2>
        <p>
          Beyond the three main criteria, some signals should always alert a manager, even when the file looks simple on the surface:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A strategic or sensitive customer</strong> — even a minor decision can have a major impact on the relationship</li>
          <li><strong>An urgent but unusual file</strong> — urgency never justifies bypassing approval</li>
          <li><strong>No clear precedent</strong> — if nobody on the team has handled this case before, it goes up</li>
          <li><strong>A disagreement between two departments</strong> — a conflict across departments is always an escalation signal</li>
          <li><strong>A possible impact on several teams</strong> — work that crosses teams justifies a manager coordinating it</li>
          <li><strong>A decision that is hard to undo</strong> — irreversible decisions are approved one level up</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-gray-700 font-medium">
            <strong>How the additional signals work:</strong> one of them is enough to qualify the file as "non-standard". They do not need to add up — each one on its own takes the file out of standard handling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>What this means for the organisation</h2>
        <p>
          Defining what counts as "standard" is not a bureaucratic exercise — it is an investment in how smoothly and how safely decisions get made. Organisations that have clarified this framework gain three concrete advantages:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>More operational autonomy</strong> — teams handle what is theirs with confidence</li>
          <li><strong>Less pointless over-escalation</strong> — managers focus on what genuinely deserves their attention</li>
          <li><strong>Better traceability</strong> — risky decisions are documented and their approval is explicit</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In practice this framework takes the form of a <strong>file qualification reference</strong>: a simple tool — one page, a grid, a checklist — that anyone can consult before deciding whether to handle a file or escalate it.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-gray-700 font-medium mb-0">
            <strong>Do your teams know when to escalate?</strong><br/>
            PerfEco works with executive teams to build clear decision references, well-calibrated delegations, and approval routes that protect without slowing things down.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="bg-navy text-white py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Let's build your decision reference</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-200 mb-8">In 60 minutes, we identify where decision-making is unclear in your organisation and lay the groundwork for a qualification framework that works.</p>
